WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.931
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.931 --> 00:00:12.958
The idea that organizations and companies
don't handle change all that well is just

00:00:12.958 --> 00:00:18.200
something that's really in the background
of a lot of organizational research.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.550
Say we go to some huge
company tomorrow and

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.930
try to understand what's
keeping them from adapting.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.728
It's very difficult, as a scientist,
to really know what's happening here and

00:00:27.728 --> 00:00:29.664
how do I measure these different things?

00:00:29.664 --> 00:00:32.810
And one evening I was just watching
a video game tournament, and

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.070
it just kind of hit me that everything
is observable in that world,

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.660
that things change relatively
often in that world.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.863
When I just encountered that
eSports setting there was

00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:45.245
just kind of a big a-ha moment.

00:00:47.957 --> 00:00:52.714
The video game that I'm looking at is a
team video game where teams of five people

00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:57.118
just have to play together in a really
coordinated way to win against other

00:00:57.118 --> 00:00:57.830
teams.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.370
You want something that has
an organizational character to it, and

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:01.230
that is coordinated.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.670
So I mean, there are lots of
video games that are individual.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:05.070
That wasn't really interesting to me.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:06.940
The other aspect had to do with the data.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.590
The fact that the data is
just available in that game.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.470
Every mouse click is being recorded,
which is, if you like to play with data,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.310
is pretty amazing.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:19.100
There was also the fact that players are
playing for millions of dollars, which is

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.920
important just to make a reasonable
assumption that those people are actually

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.370
trying to win, they're actually trying
their best to be high performing.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.950
We can be pretty confident that
whatever we're seeing here

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.950
has to do with just people trying
to coordinate with each other.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:35.147
And as a scientist, being able to
isolate that, it's just pretty cool.

00:01:40.415 --> 00:01:42.830
Organizations get faced by
change all the time and

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.990
they have new technologies
that become available.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.070
I think very often the people who
make decision those companies

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.910
tend to think of new technologies,
new tools as a way

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.610
of improving what they're doing right now,
just doing the same thing better.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.680
And that's pretty much the exact same
thing that I found in those video games.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.520
What's changing in the game are the powers
of the different heroes that

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.090
they're playing.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:14.470
Their first reaction would be to just do
the exact same thing, just with new tools.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.000
They would actually explore even less
than when you didn't have change.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.493
They wanted to relearn their current
strategy with those new tools.

00:02:23.493 --> 00:02:28.098
Most of the teams really quickly adapt
each of the pieces, but it didn't really

00:02:28.098 --> 00:02:32.790
think about how that enabled them
to just play differently as a team.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:37.435
You could argue that it would make
sense before doing any type of change,

00:02:37.435 --> 00:02:41.615
you would try to get the fundamentals
right trying to understand how that change

00:02:41.615 --> 00:02:43.765
helps us do what we're
doing right now better.

00:02:43.765 --> 00:02:44.925
What I saw in the data is that

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:49.965
teams on average tended to forget
about that second part, right?

00:02:49.965 --> 00:02:53.835
They got the basics right, and then they
just stayed with the business as is.

00:02:53.835 --> 00:02:56.185
You could make an argument that it
makes sense just because it makes you

00:02:56.185 --> 00:02:56.755
more efficient.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.060
What I saw in the data is that those
teams actually tended to lose more.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.890
The teams that did adapt more were those
where everybody was communicating together

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:10.850
and really kind of seemed to share how
the change was affecting them personally,

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:14.050
so that they can kind of brainstorm
about what they could do as a team

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:15.162
to address that.

00:03:15.162 --> 00:03:19.490
There's also a lot of changes
that are more indirect,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.020
that have to do one slow component of what
you're doing changing a little bit, and

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.128
that having ripple effects on all
of the rest of what you're doing.

00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:29.050
The winners tended to be those teams that

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.650
at least didn't seem to assume Ttat
because the change looks small,

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.880
the adaptation that we should
make will be small as well.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.070
That sometimes you had these very with
seemingly minor changes in the code of

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.885
the game,
in the heroes that were selectable, but

00:03:45.885 --> 00:03:49.107
they still just try to play in
a completely different way.

00:03:54.532 --> 00:03:59.368
So in the video game world, it really
seemed like the leaders that weren't

00:03:59.368 --> 00:04:03.902
communicating too much and
were letting their teammates participate

00:04:03.902 --> 00:04:08.280
through decision-making were
the ones that had the most success,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:13.420
were the ones most likely to look for
these new ways of working together.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.040
Whereas the ones that the only
one speaking tended to

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.940
be in those teams where you would
see this very local reasoning.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.890
Which suggests that really kind of
crowdsourcing some wisdom from your

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.610
employees seems to work quite well.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.658
Now, one thing to keep in
mind is that in those teams,

00:04:30.658 --> 00:04:32.853
the people who are the leaders
are also players, right?

00:04:32.853 --> 00:04:34.817
So you could make an argument
that is just harder for

00:04:34.817 --> 00:04:36.102
them to think of the big picture.

00:04:36.102 --> 00:04:40.022
Maybe people in companies that
are managers, and it's their entire job

00:04:40.022 --> 00:04:44.510
to be a manager, might have a better
visibility over the whole team.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:48.730
But I think there is an argument to be
made for the fact that they would still

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.890
benefit from trying to understand how
their employees work together, and

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.820
actually asking them how they
think change is affecting them and

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:57.860
how that made impact
how they work together.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.451
Doing the incremental
effort of thinking okay,

00:05:00.451 --> 00:05:04.682
how does that impact the entire team, how
does it impact not just that person but

00:05:04.682 --> 00:05:08.725
how that person can work with others,
maybe how even our unit is working with

00:05:08.725 --> 00:05:13.177
the other units in the organization, I
think can yield a lot of payoff, actually.

00:05:18.511 --> 00:05:22.832
One piece of the paper that should
make us hopeful is that when I was

00:05:22.832 --> 00:05:27.157
comparing the teams at the beginning
of the observation period to

00:05:27.157 --> 00:05:31.949
those that had gone through several
changes and had just aged over time,

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:35.032
the latter ones actually
seemed to do better.

00:05:35.032 --> 00:05:39.769
Over time, it seemed like the teams that
had those sort of changes just seemed to

00:05:39.769 --> 00:05:43.100
know that adapting system
maybe had some value.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.420
So over time, teams just got better at
adapting, not just at the local level, but

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:48.180
at the system level.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.790
Which kind of suggests that
this can be learned, right?

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.283
You're not doomed to always adapt locally.

00:05:54.283 --> 00:06:02.045
[MUSIC]

